WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.271
Agent-Monitor helps you find the agents in an
estate, understand who owns them, see

2
00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:09.256
their tools and knowledge, and decide what needs
a closer look.

3
00:00:09.256 --> 00:00:12.516
This is a page-by-page guide to the current app.

4
00:00:12.516 --> 00:00:17.950
It is not an installation walkthrough, a
security assessment, or a demonstration of live
telemetry.

5
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:24.203
The screens come from the current app running
locally with wholly synthetic fixtures adapted

6
00:00:24.203 --> 00:00:25.776
from its public demo.

7
00:00:25.776 --> 00:00:29.314
Every agent, person, site and detection shown is
fictional.

8
00:00:29.314 --> 00:00:31.279
The voice is AI generated.

9
00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:35.210
The public demo is an older version with extra
pages.

10
00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:41.500
Here we cover the current inventory and
governance app, not those retired usage and cost
views.

11
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:45.581
Look at the data chip in the top right.

12
00:00:45.581 --> 00:00:48.878
It says Sample data and gives the snapshot date.

13
00:00:48.878 --> 00:00:53.274
These examples use a fixed third of November
snapshot, not today's activity.

14
00:00:53.274 --> 00:00:56.938
In a connected installation the same area
identifies live mode.

15
00:00:56.938 --> 00:01:02.433
A recent-looking number is not enough: check its
source and date before you use it.

16
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.102
Environment, platform, risk band and agent
origin narrow the agent views.

17
00:01:08.102 --> 00:01:13.449
Here the environment filter reduces the list
from twenty-four agents to six.

18
00:01:13.449 --> 00:01:17.014
That is a smaller scope, not an improvement.

19
00:01:17.014 --> 00:01:19.242
Filters carry between agent pages.

20
00:01:19.242 --> 00:01:21.470
Clear them before comparing totals.

21
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:28.600
SharePoint, Foundry, Shadow AI and coverage have
their own scope rather than inheriting these
agent filters.

22
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:31.679
Overview is the starting brief.

23
00:01:31.679 --> 00:01:36.767
The estate card splits agents into human-built,
platform-provisioned and creator unknown.

24
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:43.704
In this fictional fixture all twenty-four are
human-built; that is not a typical tenant
benchmark.

25
00:01:43.704 --> 00:01:50.179
The neighbouring cards highlight sharing, owner
gaps, knowledge reach, inactivity and high
heuristic risk.

26
00:01:50.179 --> 00:01:55.267
They describe the loaded inventory, not business
value or successful conversations.

27
00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:59.026
What needs attention separates exposure checks
from housekeeping.

28
00:01:59.026 --> 00:02:05.013
Select a line to see the agents behind it,
rather than acting on the headline alone.

29
00:02:05.013 --> 00:02:09.876
The growth chart can show a running total or new
agents per month.

30
00:02:09.876 --> 00:02:15.114
A rising line means more inventoried agents by
creation month; it does not prove

31
00:02:15.114 --> 00:02:17.733
adoption or an increase in historical risk.

32
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:21.073
All agents is the searchable registry.

33
00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:24.458
Activity and needs-attention controls narrow the
list further.

34
00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:30.383
The first table puts activity, platform, risk,
sharing and last-use dates beside each agent.

35
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:33.769
Select an agent row to open its details.

36
00:02:33.769 --> 00:02:39.694
A blank date on a platform without a usage
signal must not be interpreted

37
00:02:39.694 --> 00:02:42.233
as proof that nobody used it.

38
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:48.880
Further down, Data and tools compares each
agent's knowledge types, connectors and
component counts.

39
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.891
Environment, ownership and sharing brings
together its location, owner, creator and reach.

40
00:02:53.891 --> 00:02:58.902
Use these tables to find the right reviewer and
understand the configuration.

41
00:02:58.902 --> 00:03:05.583
An owner name that did not resolve is different
from an agent with no owner recorded.

42
00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:10.089
The agent drawer joins the facts for one
selected row.

43
00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:14.183
At the top are risk, activity, lifecycle and the
suggested verdict.

44
00:03:14.183 --> 00:03:18.278
Under Details are the environment, dates, owner,
authentication, model and sharing.

45
00:03:18.278 --> 00:03:23.489
This is where you check whether the selected
agent is actually the one you

46
00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:26.467
meant to review, especially when names are
similar.

47
00:03:27.283 --> 00:03:31.399
Why it is risky lists the individual factors and
their points.

48
00:03:31.399 --> 00:03:35.889
Knowledge and Connectors show the related
sources and bindings that were loaded.

49
00:03:35.889 --> 00:03:39.630
Use that detail to prepare a question for the
owner.

50
00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:44.494
This drawer does not disable an agent, change
its sharing, or reassign ownership.

51
00:03:44.494 --> 00:03:48.983
Any approved change happens in the appropriate
administration tool, outside this app.

52
00:03:49.783 --> 00:03:54.606
Agent inventory emphasises creators, models and
regions, followed by a detailed register.

53
00:03:54.606 --> 00:04:00.231
Use it when the question is what was built,
where it lives and how

54
00:04:00.231 --> 00:04:01.437
it is configured.

55
00:04:01.437 --> 00:04:05.054
The human-built total is distinct from the total
estate.

56
00:04:05.054 --> 00:04:11.483
Platform-created scaffolding can otherwise
overwhelm a ranking that you intended to use to
understand people's work.

57
00:04:12.283 --> 00:04:17.646
The register adds orchestration, authentication,
risk score, component counts and dates to each
row.

58
00:04:17.646 --> 00:04:23.008
Sort or search it to find a manageable review
sample, then open a row

59
00:04:23.008 --> 00:04:24.157
for its reasons.

60
00:04:24.157 --> 00:04:29.137
A large tool count is a configuration footprint,
not proof of frequent execution.

61
00:04:29.137 --> 00:04:33.733
A named model does not tell you how good the
answers are.

62
00:04:34.533 --> 00:04:40.242
Creators and ownership shows who builds agents
and who is responsible for them.

63
00:04:40.242 --> 00:04:43.754
Creator rankings help locate experience and
support needs.

64
00:04:43.754 --> 00:04:45.950
Owner rankings help route follow-up.

65
00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:52.097
Those are different questions: the person who
first created an agent may not be

66
00:04:52.097 --> 00:04:53.414
its current owner.

67
00:04:53.414 --> 00:04:58.683
Check the origin filter before treating
platform-generated inventory as someone's
personal portfolio.

68
00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:04.852
Owner-resolution information and the ownership
tables help distinguish missing ownership from a
name that

69
00:05:04.852 --> 00:05:06.395
could not be resolved.

70
00:05:06.395 --> 00:05:11.411
In a live app, names come from users known to
the landing environment.

71
00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:13.341
That directory can be incomplete.

72
00:05:13.341 --> 00:05:18.742
Confirm responsibility with the relevant team
before describing an agent as abandoned or
deciding

73
00:05:18.742 --> 00:05:19.900
to remove it.

74
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:25.154
SharePoint agents is a separate inventory of
observed dot-agent files.

75
00:05:25.154 --> 00:05:29.162
It distinguishes current files from files
observed as deleted.

76
00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:34.506
Site, deletion state, source application and
last operation are this page's controls.

77
00:05:34.506 --> 00:05:39.851
The view is reconstructed from audit file
operations within the available lookback.

78
00:05:39.851 --> 00:05:44.750
An agent with no operation in that window may be
invisible.

79
00:05:45.533 --> 00:05:49.100
Current agents by site helps choose where to
look.

80
00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:54.648
The lower tables show each observed file and its
latest audit provenance, including the

81
00:05:54.648 --> 00:05:56.233
last operation and actor.

82
00:05:56.233 --> 00:05:59.007
These are file observations, not conversation
counts.

83
00:05:59.007 --> 00:06:04.556
Check the collection freshness and available
audit history before asking a site owner to

84
00:06:04.556 --> 00:06:06.933
reconcile a missing or deleted entry.

85
00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.778
Risk and review groups agents by a simple point
model.

86
00:06:11.778 --> 00:06:15.040
No authentication and quarantine each add three
points.

87
00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.671
Tenant-wide sharing adds two.

88
00:06:16.671 --> 00:06:19.117
Other configuration factors add smaller amounts.

89
00:06:19.117 --> 00:06:23.603
High means five or more points, medium means
three or four.

90
00:06:23.603 --> 00:06:29.311
These are review priorities defined by this
project, not Microsoft's risk rating and not

91
00:06:29.311 --> 00:06:31.350
a prediction of a breach.

92
00:06:32.117 --> 00:06:36.882
Here is an illustrative calculation, not a
result read from this dashboard.

93
00:06:36.882 --> 00:06:41.647
An agent with no authentication adds three
points; tenant-wide sharing adds two.

94
00:06:41.647 --> 00:06:46.413
That makes five before any other factors, so the
band is High.

95
00:06:46.413 --> 00:06:52.767
A connector or web-search flag is a reason to
investigate context, not evidence that data
escaped.

96
00:06:53.533 --> 00:06:59.175
The factor breakdown shows how many agents each
factor affects and how many points it
contributes.

97
00:06:59.175 --> 00:07:04.111
One agent can appear under several factors, so
do not add those rows as

98
00:07:04.111 --> 00:07:05.874
if they were distinct agents.

99
00:07:05.874 --> 00:07:08.695
The review table starts with High and Medium.

100
00:07:08.695 --> 00:07:13.983
Open a row, check its reasons and agree a
proportionate next step with its owner.

101
00:07:14.783 --> 00:07:18.908
Lifecycle review separates a usage stage from a
suggested decision.

102
00:07:18.908 --> 00:07:21.796
New agents are under thirty days old.

103
00:07:21.796 --> 00:07:27.571
Active means used within thirty days; idle is
thirty-one to ninety days, and dormant

104
00:07:27.571 --> 00:07:29.221
is longer than ninety.

105
00:07:29.221 --> 00:07:34.171
Never used applies only where the platform
supplies a usable last-use signal.

106
00:07:34.171 --> 00:07:37.883
These thresholds are project heuristics, not
universal retention policy.

107
00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:42.726
Agent Builder and Foundry have no supported
last-use signal here.

108
00:07:42.726 --> 00:07:47.959
They are not marked idle, dormant or due to
retire on that basis.

109
00:07:47.959 --> 00:07:53.596
A dormant agent, or an eligible never-used agent
at least sixty days old, receives

110
00:07:53.596 --> 00:07:54.803
a Retire suggestion.

111
00:07:54.803 --> 00:07:58.427
High risk or no owner can instead trigger
Review.

112
00:07:58.427 --> 00:08:02.050
These are prompts to confirm facts, never
automatic deletion.

113
00:08:02.867 --> 00:08:08.379
The action table hides Keep by default and puts
the more urgent verdicts first.

114
00:08:08.379 --> 00:08:12.317
Read Why and Stage alongside the owner and
last-used date.

115
00:08:12.317 --> 00:08:16.254
The cadence panel suggests when to return to
each verdict.

116
00:08:16.254 --> 00:08:20.979
Before retiring anything, ask about seasonal
use, business dependency and data completeness.

117
00:08:20.979 --> 00:08:24.917
The app records no approval and performs no
retirement itself.

118
00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:32.102
Tools brings together connector links, distinct
connectors, Model Context Protocol servers,
channels and component footprints.

119
00:08:32.102 --> 00:08:37.650
A connector link is a binding in the
configuration, not an API call.

120
00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:44.052
One agent may have several links, and a
connector may be used by several agents.

121
00:08:44.052 --> 00:08:49.600
The chart labelled Most used connectors ranks
configured links, not observed runtime traffic.

122
00:08:50.367 --> 00:08:54.548
The largest-tool-surface table compares tools,
connectors, knowledge and connected agents.

123
00:08:54.548 --> 00:09:00.403
Start with a broad footprint when you want to
understand reach, then inspect the

124
00:09:00.403 --> 00:09:05.003
selected agent and the actual connector
permissions in its administration tools.

125
00:09:05.003 --> 00:09:10.858
Presence here does not establish effective
access to every record, nor show what the

126
00:09:10.858 --> 00:09:13.367
agent has actually read or changed.

127
00:09:14.117 --> 00:09:20.451
Knowledge and grounding groups bindings by
source type, such as SharePoint sites or public
websites.

128
00:09:20.451 --> 00:09:25.942
It contrasts grounded and ungrounded agents and
separates tenant-data bindings from external
types.

129
00:09:25.942 --> 00:09:28.898
Grounded means a source binding was returned.

130
00:09:28.898 --> 00:09:34.810
It does not certify the source's quality, the
answer's accuracy, or whether the agent

131
00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:37.767
can access every item behind that source.

132
00:09:38.533 --> 00:09:43.910
Use the platform matrix and heavily grounded
agents to find where more investigation is
worthwhile.

133
00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:49.646
The counts here can be richer than the list of
named sources on the next page.

134
00:09:49.646 --> 00:09:54.665
In particular, Agent Builder exposes grounding
types but not the named-source collection used
here.

135
00:09:54.665 --> 00:09:59.683
Do not treat a gap between those two views as
proof of a fault.

136
00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:06.499
Knowledge sources adds actual source names and
locators where the collection lane supports
them.

137
00:10:06.499 --> 00:10:12.548
It shows named-source totals, hosts, external
references, freshness and coverage against the
inventory's counts.

138
00:10:12.548 --> 00:10:17.733
All locators in this film use the reserved
example dot invalid domain.

139
00:10:17.733 --> 00:10:23.350
They are fictional references, not real sites
and not links to private content.

140
00:10:24.117 --> 00:10:30.727
Our fictional example has twelve named rows
against sixty counted sources, so coverage is
twenty percent.

141
00:10:30.727 --> 00:10:34.446
That is illustrative collection coverage, not an
accuracy score.

142
00:10:34.446 --> 00:10:39.404
Use widely shared sources to find references
worth reviewing with their owners.

143
00:10:39.404 --> 00:10:45.188
If rows are absent or stale, check the knowledge
collector and environment coverage before

144
00:10:45.188 --> 00:10:47.667
concluding that agents have no knowledge.

145
00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:55.636
Azure AI Foundry lists the returned Azure AI
resources and projects, alongside Power Platform
agent flows.

146
00:10:55.636 --> 00:10:59.678
Resource categories, regions and stock-keeping
units describe the estate.

147
00:10:59.678 --> 00:11:06.863
A Foundry project can host multiple agents; the
control-plane project count is not an
individual-agent count.

148
00:11:06.863 --> 00:11:13.600
The agent filters from earlier pages do not
apply to these resource and flow totals.

149
00:11:14.367 --> 00:11:18.950
The estate table helps locate the resource and
region to review.

150
00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:23.950
Public network open counts only resources whose
returned property explicitly says Enabled.

151
00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:30.617
A live listing may omit that property, so a
blank is not confirmation of private access.

152
00:11:30.617 --> 00:11:36.450
Use the source administration tools to check the
actual network and authentication configuration
before

153
00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:38.117
making a security decision.

154
00:11:38.867 --> 00:11:43.182
Shadow AI presents watched-tool observations
associated with devices and users.

155
00:11:43.182 --> 00:11:46.204
Layer and posture filters narrow the results.

156
00:11:46.204 --> 00:11:53.109
The tools and observations shown here are
fictional fixtures, not findings about any
vendor or organisation.

157
00:11:53.109 --> 00:11:59.582
In a connected installation the signal depends
on Defender coverage and the project's matching
rules.

158
00:11:59.582 --> 00:12:03.467
It is not a complete record of AI use.

159
00:12:04.283 --> 00:12:10.697
The device table lets a reviewer inspect the
reported tool, layer, user and last-seen date.

160
00:12:10.697 --> 00:12:16.683
The watchlist shows what the app looks for, with
sanctioning defined by local policy.

161
00:12:16.683 --> 00:12:20.104
Installed, executed and network observations
mean different things.

162
00:12:20.104 --> 00:12:25.235
Do not add them as unique devices or infer a
data leak.

163
00:12:25.235 --> 00:12:29.083
Unmanaged devices and uncollected activity
remain outside the evidence.

164
00:12:29.867 --> 00:12:34.180
Data sources and gaps explains the boundaries of
the product.

165
00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:38.925
Telemetry per platform tells you where inventory
and last-use signals exist.

166
00:12:38.925 --> 00:12:44.965
The API investigation table and optional Agent
Three Sixty-Five comparison document known
capability gaps.

167
00:12:44.965 --> 00:12:51.867
These are reference information, not a fresh
permissions test performed each time you open
this page.

168
00:12:52.617 --> 00:12:58.178
Use this page when a stakeholder asks for a
metric the app cannot support.

169
00:12:58.178 --> 00:13:03.739
Conversation-level usage, people, value and cost
are outside the current app's remit; use the

170
00:13:03.739 --> 00:13:06.917
appropriate analytics product rather than
guessing from inventory.

171
00:13:06.917 --> 00:13:12.478
Before presenting any total, be clear about
which platforms and sources it covers and

172
00:13:12.478 --> 00:13:14.067
which questions remain unanswered.

173
00:13:14.867 --> 00:13:20.135
Live lane status shows each independent data
lane, its connector, state and row count,

174
00:13:20.135 --> 00:13:21.640
plus collector run information.

175
00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:26.532
In this film every lane is marked Sample because
the data is synthetic.

176
00:13:26.532 --> 00:13:29.919
We have not connected live services or run
collectors.

177
00:13:29.919 --> 00:13:35.188
This page's title describes its role in the
product, not a claim that the

178
00:13:35.188 --> 00:13:36.317
recording is live.

179
00:13:37.117 --> 00:13:42.781
In a connected app, distinguish returned data,
no rows, not set up, access denied

180
00:13:42.781 --> 00:13:43.995
and an error.

181
00:13:43.995 --> 00:13:47.232
Read the detail and collector timestamps as
well.

182
00:13:47.232 --> 00:13:52.493
A missing lane should remain an explicit gap,
not become a comforting zero.

183
00:13:52.493 --> 00:13:58.967
Reload after resolving the underlying issue,
then check whether the expected environments and
sources are represented.

184
00:13:59.783 --> 00:14:03.997
Ask opens a side panel for questions about the
current inventory.

185
00:14:03.997 --> 00:14:09.359
It builds a summary of counts, ownership gaps,
risk, lifecycle, integrations and source
coverage,

186
00:14:09.359 --> 00:14:11.274
including the page and filters.

187
00:14:11.274 --> 00:14:13.573
It adds no new inventory query.

188
00:14:13.573 --> 00:14:18.935
In a supported player with Cowork enabled, that
question and summary can be sent

189
00:14:18.935 --> 00:14:21.233
to Copilot under the user's account.

190
00:14:22.033 --> 00:14:27.076
Outside that player, the panel offers a
question-and-summary handoff to copy into
Copilot Chat.

191
00:14:27.076 --> 00:14:29.597
That is the actual state shown here.

192
00:14:29.597 --> 00:14:32.838
What gets sent lets you inspect the text first.

193
00:14:32.838 --> 00:14:37.881
We do not submit it to a live AI service in this
film, and

194
00:14:37.881 --> 00:14:39.682
we show no invented answer.

195
00:14:39.682 --> 00:14:43.283
Review the content and your organisation's
policies before sharing it.

196
00:14:44.033 --> 00:14:46.987
Start with the date, mode and coverage.

197
00:14:46.987 --> 00:14:52.894
Use Overview to choose a concern, the registry
to find the agent, and its

198
00:14:52.894 --> 00:14:55.004
drawer to read the reasons.

199
00:14:55.004 --> 00:15:00.911
Governance helps prioritise a human review;
integrations and resource pages explain the
surrounding footprint.

200
00:15:00.911 --> 00:15:04.708
Confirm missing information with the owner and
source administrator.

201
00:15:04.708 --> 00:15:08.083
A colour or score never replaces that
conversation.

202
00:15:08.867 --> 00:15:13.991
All inventory examples in this film use
fictional synthetic data, with AI-generated
narration.

203
00:15:13.991 --> 00:15:20.298
The screens show the current app; this is not
live monitoring and no agent was changed.

204
00:15:20.298 --> 00:15:25.028
Risk, lifecycle and posture labels are review
aids rather than verified outcomes.

205
00:15:25.028 --> 00:15:30.546
Use the chapters to return to a page, then apply
the same careful reading

206
00:15:30.546 --> 00:15:32.517
to your own authorised data.
